<GuideLayout
  slug="post-construction-window-cleaning"
  title="Post-Construction Window Cleaning: New Home & Remodel Guide"
  description="Post-construction window cleaning: removing stickers, film adhesive, drywall dust, paint, and mortar from new windows without scratching the glass."
  heading="Post-Construction Window Cleaning: What a New Home Needs"
  lede="New windows come with stickers, film, dust, and paint specks. Removing them without scratching brand-new glass takes a different approach than a regular cleaning."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>Whether it's a new build in Independence or a remodel in Fort Mitchell, construction leaves a layer of residue on every window. Getting it off is straightforward with the right steps — and easy to get wrong in ways that permanently scratch new glass.</p>

  <h2>What's Usually on the Glass</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Manufacturer stickers and labels</strong> — and the adhesive left behind when they're half-peeled.</li>
    <li><strong>Protective film</strong> on some windows and doors. Sunlight bakes the adhesive on if it's left too long.</li>
    <li><strong>Drywall and sanding dust</strong> on interior glass, frames, and packed into tracks.</li>
    <li><strong>Paint specks and overspray</strong> from trim and wall painting.</li>
    <li><strong>Mortar, stucco, or siding debris</strong> on exterior glass near masonry work.</li>
    <li><strong>Caulk and sealant smears</strong> along frames.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How Post-Construction Window Cleaning Is Done</h2>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Rinse first.</strong> Construction dust is abrasive, so the glass is soaked and rinsed before anything touches it.</li>
    <li><strong>Remove stickers and adhesive</strong> by soaking them and using an adhesive remover, with a plastic scraper before any blade.</li>
    <li><strong>Soften and lift paint</strong> using the wet-blade method — see the <a href="/guides/removing-paint-from-window-glass/">paint removal guide</a>. New windows are often tempered, which calls for extra caution with blades.</li>
    <li><strong>Treat mortar splatter</strong> by soaking it until it softens rather than scraping it dry. Fresh masonry residue is alkaline and can etch glass if left for a long time.</li>
    <li><strong>Vacuum and wipe the tracks and sills</strong>, where most of the debris ends up.</li>
    <li><strong>Final wash and inspection</strong> of every pane, inside and out, in good light.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Why scratches happen on new windows:</strong> tempered glass can have tiny particles fused to its surface from the manufacturing process. A blade that catches one drags it across the pane. Testing a corner first and using solvents instead of blades wherever possible avoids most of these scratches.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Timing It Right</h2>

  <p>The best time is after the last dusty trade is finished and before you move furniture in — ladders and poles are much easier to use in an empty house. If you're closing on a new build, book the cleaning for the days between closing and move-in.</p>

  <h2>What It Costs</h2>

  <p>Post-construction cleaning usually runs two to three times a normal cleaning because every pane needs detailed residue removal. See the <a href="/guides/window-cleaning-cost/">window cleaning cost guide</a> for normal ranges to compare against.</p>
</GuideLayout>
